Research Overview for "Hyperplane families creating envelopes":

Let $N$ be an $n$-dimensional $C^\infty$ manifold and let $\widetilde{\varphi}: N	o \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$, $\widetilde{
u}: N	o S^n$ be $C^\infty$ mappings. We first give a necessary and sufficient condition for the hyperplane family $\mathcal{H}_{(\widetilde{\varphi}, \widetilde{
u})}$ defined by $\mathcal{H}_{(\widetilde{\varphi}, \widetilde{
u})} =\cup_{x\in N}\left\{X\in \mathbb{R}^{n+1}\; |\; \left(X-\widetilde{\varphi}(x)ight)\cdot \widetilde{
u}(x)=0ight\}$ to create an envelope (Theorem 1). As a by-product of the proof of Theorem 1, when the given hyperplane family $\mathcal{H}_{(\widetilde{\varphi}, \widetilde{
u})}$ creates an envelope $\widetilde{f}: N	o \mathbb{R}^{n+1}$, an explicit expression of the envelope $\widetilde{f}$ is obtained in terms of $\widetilde{\varphi}$ and $\widetilde{
u}$ (Corollary 2). The vector formula given in Corollary 2 holds even at a singular point of $\widetilde{
u}$ so long as the hyperplane family $\mathcal{H}_{(\widetilde{\varphi}, \widetilde{
u})}$ creates an envelope. In this sense, Corollary 2 may be regarded as a complete generalization of the celebrated Cahn-Hoffman vector formula. Moreover, we give a criterion when and only when $\mathcal{H}_{\left(\widetilde{\varphi}, \widetilde{
u}ight)}$ creates a unique envelope (Theorem 2).

